  "raw": {
    "https://plus.avanti.ca/job-board/5da7a070-4efb-4f6e-b846-5d1e55cc2abe/d38e5bd7-5bd3-4438-a9f4-0699f15b861e/view?LOCALE=en-CA&SEARCH=UWFA-RAS": {
      "title": "Probationary Tenure-Track Assistant Professor in Cognitive Psychology, Department of Psychology",
      "description": "Avanti English (CA) Any location Any job type Jobs (4) Probationary Tenure-Track Assistant Professor in Cognitive Psychology, Department of Psychology - UWFA-RAS Job ID: 95 In-person (Winnipeg, MB) | Full time Apply Probationary Faculty, Indigenous Languages Program, Department of Anthropology - UWFA-RAS Job ID: 48 In-person (Winnipeg, MB) | Full time Apply Probationary Instructors, Indigenous Languages Program, Department of Anthropology - UWFA-RAS Job ID: 47 In-person (Winnipeg, MB) | Full time Apply Tenure Track Assistant Professor in Developmental Studies, Faculty of Education - UWFA-RAS Job ID: 38 In-person (Winnipeg, MB) | Full time Apply 1 Job details Apply Probationary Tenure-Track Assistant Professor in Cognitive Psychology, Department of Psychology - UWFA-RAS Job ID: 95 In-person (Winnipeg, MB) | Full time Accepting applications until 28 Sep 2026 Description DEPARTMENT OF PSYCHOLOGY The Department of Psychology at the University of Winnipeg invites applications for a probationary tenure-track faculty position at the Assistant Professor level in Cognitive Psychology . The earliest start date for the appointment would be July 1, 2027, subject to budgetary approval.  See our Collective Agreement for salary information ( https://www.uwinnipeg.ca/hr/docs/uwfa/uwfa-ras-2020-2027-final-2024-05-01.pdf ). The successful applicant will have a demonstrated record of research productivity with the ability to build and maintain an externally funded research program. Applicants must have completed a Ph.D. in psychology or be near completion by the start date of the appointment. The University of Winnipeg is an urban campus on Treaty One land in the heart of the Métis homeland. We are a liberal arts and sciences institution with a diverse student body and a focus on undergraduate education. The psychology department has 19 faculty members with varied research interests and offers three-year, four-year, and honours undergraduate degrees. The successful applicant will: (a) teach Cognitive Psychology or Neuroscience and related courses. The ability to teach upper-level undergraduate statistics [e.g., Factorial ANOVA and Multiple Regression] and to integrate multiple statistical software packages [including SPSS and R] into instruction is also an asset; (b) maintain an active research program that incorporates undergraduates (recent tenure-track hires have obtained tri-council funding within the first two years of appointment); and, (c) share in the administrative responsibilities of the department and university. As per our Collective Agreement, the successful applicant will receive a reduced teaching load in the first year and will be eligible for another in their second year. Additionally, faculty members who receive tri-council funding normally receive a yearly teaching reduction commensurate with their grant role. The University of Winnipeg is committed to equity, diversity and inclusion and recognizes that a diverse staff and faculty benefits and enriches the work, learning and research environments, and is essential to academic and institutional excellence. In accordance with the University of Winnipeg Employment Equity and Diversity Policy and section 11 of The Human Rights Code, this position is designated for qualified candidates who self-identify as women, racialized persons, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and 2SLGBTQ+ persons. Please confidentially self-identify at the time of application. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadian citizens and permanent residents will be given priority. Applicants are requested to submit a complete application, including all of the below documents, in a single PDF: a cover letter a curriculum vitae a research statement (e.g., research plan) a teaching statement (e.g., teaching philosophy, course outlines, formal student evaluations of teaching, and/or informal testimonials from students) the names and contact information of three individuals who can provide letters of recommendation on relatively short notice The application deadline is September 28th, 2026 . Applicants must submit the above materials through The University of Winnipeg’s online recruitment system: Avanti If you require accommodation supports during the recruitment process, please contact Elieth Martinez, HR Consultant at e.martinez@uwinnipeg.ca . Questions pertaining to the position may be directed to Harinder Aujla ( h.aujla@uwinnipeg.ca ). The personal information of applicants is collected under the authority of the University of Winnipeg Act and 36(1)(b) of the Freedom of Information and Protection of Privacy Act. All personal information collected via the recruitment process is used to assess the applicant’s suitability, eligibility, and qualifications for employment, and to otherwise support recruitment activities. This information will be provided to participating members of the recruitment process. Questions regarding the collection of your personal information may be directed to the Director, HR Services, 515 Portage Avenue, Winnipeg, MB, R3B 2E9. Date Posted: August 18, 2026 Competition Number: U21.26"
    },
    "https://plus.avanti.ca/job-board/5da7a070-4efb-4f6e-b846-5d1e55cc2abe/3c703d88-75ad-4388-a94b-33c3932b648e/view?LOCALE=en-CA&SEARCH=UWFA-RAS": {
      "title": "Probationary Faculty, Indigenous Languages Program, Department of Anthropology",
      "description": "Avanti English (CA) Any location Any job type Jobs (4) Probationary Tenure-Track Assistant Professor in Cognitive Psychology, Department of Psychology - UWFA-RAS Job ID: 95 In-person (Winnipeg, MB) | Full time Apply Probationary Faculty, Indigenous Languages Program, Department of Anthropology - UWFA-RAS Job ID: 48 In-person (Winnipeg, MB) | Full time Apply Probationary Instructors, Indigenous Languages Program, Department of Anthropology - UWFA-RAS Job ID: 47 In-person (Winnipeg, MB) | Full time Apply Tenure Track Assistant Professor in Developmental Studies, Faculty of Education - UWFA-RAS Job ID: 38 In-person (Winnipeg, MB) | Full time Apply 1 Job details Apply Probationary Faculty, Indigenous Languages Program, Department of Anthropology - UWFA-RAS Job ID: 48 In-person (Winnipeg, MB) | Full time Description The University of Winnipeg Indigenous Languages Program, Department of Anthropology Probationary Faculty in Anishinaabemowin The Anishinaabemowin Program at The University of Winnipeg invites applications for up to three probationary Faculty Member appointments (Lecturer, Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Professor). These are positions where the successful applicant(s)’s responsibilities will be teaching, research or scholarly activity, and service to the University. Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience, and in accordance with The University of Winnipeg Faculty Association Collective Agreement (2020-2027). Subject to budgetary approval, the date of appointment is anticipated to be August 1, 2026; later start dates are negotiable. The academic appointees will represent various disciplines and career stages, and will contribute to the Anishinaabemowin Program within the Indigenous Languages Program. The range of expertise we are seeking includes: Anishinaabemowin second-language teaching, including immersion methods, land-based camps, or curriculum and materials development; Indigenous language revitalization methods, including language nests and mentor apprentice programs; Indigenous language pedagogy and teacher training; Descriptive linguistics, with expertise in Algonquian Languages. We are looking for individuals with a strong commitment to Indigenous focused teaching and research/scholarship and demonstrated competency with Anishinaabemowin and Anishinaabe culture and communities. Skills related to language revitalization teaching methods and trauma informed teaching practice are an asset. Candidates should demonstrate effectiveness in teaching, as well as research and service experience, consistent with the rank they are seeking. Ideally, one of the successful candidates will be an established scholar with administrative experience able to take over coordination of the umbrella Indigenous Languages Program, and at least one of the successful candidates will be an experienced language teacher with immersion camp experience. While applicants holding or currently pursuing a Ph.D. are preferred, we also welcome applicants with other educational qualifications (at minimum a Master’s degree) and lived experience. Preference will be given to qualified candidates who identify as Indigenous. Applicants are requested to submit a complete application that will include (all documents in PDF): a cover letter outlining relevant experience in teaching, research/scholarship, and university service a current curriculum vitae a research/scholarship statement a teaching statement a statement indicating their relationships to and with Indigenous languages, cultures, and communities names and contact information of three references who can speak about the applicant’s Indigenous-specific teaching, research, and service (referees will be contacted soon after short-listing of an applicant) In the later stages of the selection process, additional steps supporting the substantiation of Indigenous identity will occur for individuals claiming Indigenous identity and who are invited to an interview with the selection committee. Applicants must submit all of the above materials through The University of Winnipeg’s online recruitment system ( Avanti+ Job Board ). Review of applications will begin on July 27, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. We appreciate all applicants’ interest but please note that only short-listed candidates will be contacted. For any queries, please contact: Dr. Julie Pelletier, Chair, Department of Anthropology, ju.pelletier@uwinnipeg.ca Located in downtown Winnipeg, Manitoba, and on original Treaty One land, in the heart of the Red River Métis homeland, The University is home to approximately 10,000 undergraduate and graduate students, 340 full-time faculty, 323 instructional staff, and 489 support staff. It has an established reputation for providing a supportive learning and working environment, combining a commitment to academic and research excellence, dedication to Indigenous engagement and success, and connections with diverse communities. The Indigenous Languages Program offers Certificates, a minor, and Bachelor of Arts degrees (3 year, 4 year, Honours) in Indigenous Languages, Anishinaabemowin, and Teaching Indigenous Languages for Vitality. The Anishinaabemowin programs are offered through immersion cohort models, with a strong emphasis on community-building and experiential learning. For more information about the Indigenous Languages program and Anishinaabemowin Immersion cohorts, please contact Aandeg Muldrew aa.muldrew@uwinnipeg.ca . The University of Winnipeg is committed to equity, diversity and inclusion and recognizes that a diverse staff and faculty benefits and enriches the work, learning and research environments, and is essential to academic and institutional excellence. We welcome applications from all qualified individuals and encourage women, racialized persons, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and 2SLGBTQ+ persons to confidentially self-identify at time of application. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadian citizens and permanent residents will be given priority. Additional information on the University of Winnipeg is available at http://www.uwinnipeg.ca/ . The University of Winnipeg is committed to ensuring employment opportunities are accessible for all applicants. If you require accommodation supports during the recruitment process, please contact human_resources@uwinnipeg.ca . The personal information of applicants is collected under the authority of the University of Winnipeg Act and 36(1)(b) of the Freedom of Information and Protection of Privacy Act. All personal information collected via the recruitment process is used to assess the applicant’s suitability, eligibility, and qualifications for employment, and to otherwise support recruitment activities. This information will be provided to participating members of the recruitment process. Questions regarding the collection of your personal information may be directed to the Director, HR Services, 515 Portage Avenue, Winnipeg, MB, R3B 2E9 or human_resources@uwinnipeg.ca. Date Posted: July 20, 2026 Competition Number: U19.29"
    },
